1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,390
The course of true love never did run smooth, Lysander tells us in Shakespeare's A Midsummer Night's Dream.
莱桑德在莎士比亚的《仲夏夜之梦》中说道：“真爱之路从不平坦。”

2
00:00:07,390 --> 00:00:10,410
Wise words, many would say.
很多人都会说，这是至理名言。

3
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:17,680
In the fast-paced world of modern life, it can be difficult to meet anyone, let alone our soulmate.
在快节奏的现代生活中，遇见一个人都很难，更别说遇见灵魂伴侣了。

4
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:25,370
Fortunately, there's an app for that, though wooing a potential partner is still up to you.
幸运的是，有一款应用可以帮你解决这个问题，不过追求潜在伴侣仍然要靠你自己。

5
00:00:25,370 --> 00:00:35,830
Dating apps like Tinder, Bumble and Chappy have been around for a good few years and statistics prove their ongoing popularity.
Tinder、Bumble和Chappy等约会应用已经存在好几年了，数据证明它们一直很受欢迎。

6
00:00:35,830 --> 00:00:40,420
Tinder claims to get 1.6 billion swipes per day.
Tinder声称每天的滑动次数达16亿次。

7
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:55,420
A study conducted by globaldatinginsights.com, analysts of the online dating industry, found that lonely hearts in the UK spent 10 hours a week using them to find someone to go out with.
在线约会行业分析机构globaldatinginsights.com开展的一项研究发现，英国的单身人士每周会花10个小时使用这些应用寻找约会对象。

8
00:00:55,420 --> 00:01:10,970
And finally, a 2018 YouGov survey conducted on behalf of BBC Radio 1 Newsbeat found that of over 2000 16-34-year-olds, nearly half had used a dating app at some point.
最后，2018年舆观调查公司（YouGov）代表英国广播公司第一电台《新闻节拍》节目开展的一项调查显示，在2000多名16至34岁的受访者中，近一半人曾在某一时期使用过约会应用。

9
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:18,880
That said, the very same survey found that only 17% had met their current beau on an app.
尽管如此，同一项调查发现，只有17%的人是在应用上遇到了现在的伴侣。

10
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:29,590
And 37% said that using an app was their least preferred method to find a potential mate to take out, for a fling or otherwise.
37%的人表示，无论是寻找约会对象、短暂恋情对象还是其他关系对象，使用应用都是他们最不喜欢的方式。

11
00:01:29,590 --> 00:01:36,230
One possible reason for this comes from one respondent, 26-year-old Jordan.
26岁的受访者乔丹给出了其中一个可能的原因。

12
00:01:36,230 --> 00:01:41,620
He believes that dating apps create issues with self-doubt and anxiety.
他认为约会应用会引发自我怀疑和焦虑问题。

13
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:48,870
"You're judged for the person you are on screen and not for the person you are," he told the BBC.
“人们评判的是屏幕上的你，而不是真实的你，”他告诉英国广播公司。

14
00:01:48,870 --> 00:01:58,960
As people open themselves up to more rejection, it can lead to feelings of depression, says relationship psychologist Madeleine Mason Roantree.
情感心理学家玛德琳·梅森·罗恩特里表示，当人们面临更多拒绝时，可能会产生抑郁情绪。

15
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:06,800
Indeed, Jordan says he's been judged on dating apps for being "too young, too old and too fat."
事实上，乔丹说自己在约会应用上曾因“太年轻、太老、太胖”而受到评判。

16
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:12,550
Deception can be another issue, with profiles often stretching the truth.
欺骗是另一个问题，用户资料往往会夸大事实。

17
00:02:12,550 --> 00:02:23,200
Twenty-year-old Emma told Newsbeat about meeting a suitor whose height matched hers on their profiles, only to find he was a foot shorter in reality.
20岁的艾玛告诉《新闻节拍》，她曾遇到一个追求者，资料上显示两人身高相同，结果发现他实际上比自己矮一英尺。

18
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:33,120
Twenty-four-year-old Mikey believes dating apps are "quite Catfish-y" after meeting someone whose profile picture did not match their appearance.
24岁的米奇在遇到一个个人资料照片与本人不符的人后，认为约会应用“很容易出现虚假信息”。

19
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:37,170
So does this mean that dating apps are a waste of time?
那么，这是否意味着约会应用是在浪费时间？

20
00:02:37,170 --> 00:02:38,700
It depends.
这取决于具体情况。

21
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:43,740
Over 25% of those asked by YouGov might not agree.
舆观调查公司（YouGov）的受访者中，超过25%的人可能不会同意这种说法。

22
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:49,020
They were going steady or had tied the knot as a result of a dating app.
他们通过约会应用确定了稳定关系，甚至步入了婚姻殿堂。

23
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:57,140
A happily ever after might only be round the corner, but one way to find out is to swipe right and see.
幸福的结局可能就在眼前，而验证这一点的方法之一就是右滑试试。
